so you're about to start a family  or you've already started one having a  child is a beautiful transcendent  experience  but you're also going to find yourself  asking how much is this going to cost me  i like him holding up this uh notebook  [Music]  this episode is personal to me bobby  here was born last year  and i often look at him and i wonder  what the future holds  not just for him but for uh me and my  wallet  what does daycare cost what do diapers  cost does he need a college fund  it's pretty daunting to add all that  stuff up but christina livederry has  actually crunched the numbers  christina thank you so much for being on  good money i  just had a baby he's nine months old so  we're kind of going through  this there's figures out there that most  people get  about what having a kid costs  and you kind of delved into that and  uh saw that it saw what the truth was i  suppose  it really does vary and depend upon a  number of factors  so a great cost that can vary a lot  is is the cost of maternity health care  on average americans can expect to spend  if they're covered by a health plan  forty five hundred dollars  for routine maternity health care  that doesn't include you know if you  have an emergency c-section  or if you have a a longer than expected  stay in the nicu  so 4 500 is really just the base  starting point  the next thing we wanted to investigate  was what we call getting ready for baby  costs  how much are we going to spend on  getting that  nursery ready most of our clients spend  about five  thousand dollars for some of those  bigger items  that don't get bought during the baby  shower so that could be your crib  your bassinet your stroller it's worth  mentioning  the baby care products market is  projected to be worth over 100 billion  dollars worldwide by 2025.  you could do parental um  or birthing classes so that's like 650  that's like on average for one of those  classes  but there's also uh doulas and lactation  consultants  on average was about 2500 for  either that lactation consultant and or  doula so then the final part  the most you'll spend on your your child  is child care  the cost of child care is enormous  very early on if you decide and you have  the means to do  a night nurse that night nurse in la can  range anywhere from thirty dollars to  forty dollars an  hour and then you also have just basic  child care so whether it's  infant daycare a nanny and  especially popular here in los angeles  is  having an au pair and so an au pair will  cost you  20 000 a year but the other thing is  that they'll also  have to live with you so if you add all  those up it comes to a grand total of  forty seven thousand dollars  whoo yeah gosh  that's the cost of having a baby right  now but what about the future  are they going to go to college trade  school when are they going to move out  of the house  ever roger ma is a certified financial  planner and he's been thinking about  this stuff a lot  especially since he has a baby of his  own name owen both of them joined us  to talk  let me just start by saying uh i i  haven't done anything to prepare for  bobby's financial future  i'm starting at zero what can i do today  to to make that a rosier picture it's  always good to get a sense of where  you're starting from so  i think coming up with your financial  report card  which includes calculating your net  worth  figuring out what your living expenses  are how much you spend on a monthly  basis yeah owen  and in what categories and then also  what your credit score is as well  and i think that's a great starting  point  is saving for college as big of a deal  now as it  used to be the cost of education has  been  increasing substantially year  after year but honestly i don't think we  really know  what the what form higher education is  going to take  in 15 20 years um  and if it's gonna be as important in  terms of uh  employers using it for signaling so i  certainly recommend  to save within a 529 plan a 529 plan is  a tax advantage  savings account designed to be used for  a beneficiary's education expenses  like bobby's let's say  uh i i do a 529 plan and uh  he grows up and he's like i don't want  to go to college  um what happens to that money yeah so um  usually  if you're taking it out for non-eligible  expenses  you might get taxed at your uh federal  tax rate  um state tax might be subject to local  tax too  and then you might pay a penalty as well  that said i think it's also important  to save within say a roth ira or a  taxable brokerage account  money in those accounts is a little more  flexible than  money in a 529 plan our education  expenses  what does that include so i believe it  includes room board  tuition uh certain electronic expenses  like computer  and some other technology there's some  flexibility to change the beneficiary in  the account so certainly  you might intend to use it for bobby's  educational expenses but let's say he  goes to college you still have money  left  and you want to take a a class for  yourself  and it's at an eligible institution you  could change the beneficiary to yourself  i think the other two things um that  would be  helpful to get in a place are  a term life insurance policy outside of  your  employer coverage and then making sure  that you have an estate plan  in place and if you already have one in  place  updating it to incorporate your little  one  and particularly not only  figuring out where your stuff goes but  in the case where you're not around and  your partner  isn't around who is going to take care  of bobby  so start a 529 plan get a term life  insurance policy outside of my employer  and change my estate planner will  to include bobby  alright so i'm starting to feel better  now that i know the cost it's probably  going to be in the  tens of thousands of dollars this is  information that i wish i'd had  a while ago and that i hope that  he has someday as well unfortunately  he's probably not going to learn this in  our traditional school system  the best example is going to be learning  from you  how does one teach kids to have a good  relationship with money  even if you don't feel like you have a  great relationship with money you have a  good understanding of your own money  situation i'm sharing your  money ideas and any experiences with  money  money is in every facet of our life  going to the grocery store  driving our cars living in our houses so  being  open and honest with our children when  you're making a grocery list  and going and looking at the ads to see  what's on sale this week  when you're talking about doing chores  in the house if you're going to give an  allowance and  why we would be doing that for our  children like why we're giving you  the allowance and just leading by  example and not being  afraid of your own insecurities around  money because  those are going to transpose onto your  children what would you suggest  is like a proper amount to divulge about  you know income um comfort  just being more open even if it's like  possibly  negative things telling your kids we  don't have enough money for that or  remember we want to go  and take this vacation next month so  this month we're not going to go out to  dinner or we're not going to go to  um chuck e cheese or to the movies we're  going to try to save our money because  we want to have money for next  our next trip our next month and just  explaining  that process to children what's actually  going on in your head i think is very  helpful because  a lot of times they don't see it they  don't understand how the money even  comes in  why daddy goes to work why mommy goes to  work um  that putting gas in the car costs money  sometimes they don't even see those  transactions  um so allowing them to see those  transactions is always  very um helpful  so we talked about expenses planning and  teaching your kids about money  now i hope that the school system starts  to make financial education a little bit  more commonplace  but until then we're gonna have to just  do it ourselves  you

